    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="e67724d3-ef96-572c-afa3-773d709aa95b" activityNameId="dea81515-5cfa-5d01-8221-018f9f59c77e" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Glider lightweighting</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Glider lightweighting
 A report from the Steel Recycling Institute (Sebastian and Thimons 2017) indicates that every kilogram of steel in a car glider can be subsituted by 0.75 kilogram of AHSS or 0.68 kilogram of aluminium. Looking at the material composition of different car models three years apart, (Hottle et al. 2017) show that steel is in fact increasingly replaced by a combination of both aluminium and AHSS. However, they also show that the use of AHSS is generally preferred to aluminium as it allows greater mass reduction at a lower cost Hence, we will consider that, for a given mass reduction to achieve, two-third comes from using AHSS, and one third comes from using aluminium. This means that one kilogram of mass reduction is achieved by replacing 3.57 kilogram of steel by: 1.76 kilogram of AHSS and 0.8 kilogram of aluminium.. Original source: Assumption - Steel Recycling Institute, Sebastian and Thimons 2017; Hottle et al. 2017, doi: 10.1016/j.trd.2017.08.010 … or Sacchi. UUID: 2bcae8e8-f381-3ec0-806f-b6b353654077
Location: Unspecified
Technology: A report from the Steel Recycling Institute (Sebastian and Thimons 2017) indicates that every kilogram of steel in a car glider can be subsituted by 0.75 kilogram of AHSS or 0.68 kilogram of aluminium. Looking at the material composition of different car models three years apart, (Hottle et al. 2017) show that steel is in fact increasingly replaced by a combination of both aluminium and AHSS. However, they also show that the use of AHSS is generally preferred to aluminium as it allows greater mass reduction at a lower cost Hence, we will consider that, for a given mass reduction to achieve, two-third comes from using AHSS, and one third comes from using aluminium. This means that one kilogram of mass reduction is achieved by replacing 3.57 kilogram of steel by: 1.76 kilogram of AHSS and 0.8 kilogram of aluminium.. Original source: Assumption - Steel Recycling Institute, Sebastian and Thimons 2017; Hottle et al. 2017, doi: 10.1016/j.trd.2017.08.010 … or Sacchi
Time period: Base technology year of 2020
Production volume: Unspecified
Sampling: Unspecified
Extrapolations: Unspecified
Uncertainty adjustments: Unspecified
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_2bcae8e8-f381-3ec0-806f-b6b353654077.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
